98
00:06:56,020 --> 00:06:57,220
What do you think

99
00:06:57,220 --> 00:07:00,740
When you put real responsibility in the hands of youth leaders,

100
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:03,460
there will be shortcomings and difficulties,

101
00:07:03,460 --> 00:07:06,420
And this is where friction starts with the parents.

102
00:07:06,420 --> 00:07:11,700
We need dent chiefs and troop guides, but it doesn't seem that there are many Scouts that want to do it.

103
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:16,580
I really don't want to force them into doing something they do not want to do

104
00:07:16,580 --> 00:07:22,420
And, by the way, I have dibs on the first graduate card of your podcast training program.

105
00:07:22,420 --> 00:07:27,300
Well, David, I'll give you a couple of quick answers to this.

106
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:31,900
Catering to the Scouts and being too accommodating of what they want to do,

107
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:33,500
I don't really understand that.

108
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:36,780
I think that that's what we're all about.

109
00:07:36,780 --> 00:07:38,900
This is about Scouts.

110
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:41,980
This is not about what adult leaders want.

111
00:07:41,980 --> 00:07:44,420
This is what Scouting is right.

112
00:07:44,420 --> 00:07:46,460
Are they doing the things that Scouts do?

113
00:07:46,460 --> 00:07:47,900
Are they planning it for themselves?

114
00:07:47,900 --> 00:07:50,820
Are they getting all those experiences

115
00:07:50,820 --> 00:07:52,820
We're doing? great, okay?

116
00:07:52,820 --> 00:07:59,620
So you're going to encounter a little friction caused by people not understanding exactly what's supposed to happen,

117
00:07:59,620 --> 00:08:02,940
And it's something that you have to keep explaining.

118
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:10,100
As far as dent chiefs and troop guides and Scouts being reluctant to take on the responsibility,

119
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:15,380
well, you're certainly not going to make them or force them to do something that they don't want to do,

120
00:08:15,380 --> 00:08:20,020
And whether or not you're going to fill those positions depends on your attitude.

121
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:25,500
If you encourage and you kind of paint a picture of the possibilities there,

122
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:33,180
you know we don't want our Scouts serving time in a position of responsibility just to get a rank.

123
00:08:33,180 --> 00:08:39,220
We want them to take on a position of responsibility because they feel like it is something important for them to do,

124
00:08:39,220 --> 00:08:42,380
Find some fulfillment in serving others.

125
00:08:42,380 --> 00:08:48,540
It's not about just plugging them into an empty position, because we really need somebody there.

126
00:08:48,579 --> 00:08:56,020
Sometimes things like that go lacking when we don't have any boys and want to be a dent chief or who want to serve as a troop guide.

127
00:08:56,020 --> 00:09:00,740
It's not that we are going to look at the boys and try and figure out what's wrong with them.

128
00:09:00,740 --> 00:09:06,699
I think we need to look at ourselves and we need to look at what's going on within the troop and answer the question:

129
00:09:06,699 --> 00:09:08,780
Why isn't anybody interested?

130
00:09:08,780 --> 00:09:17,980
Is there something that we can change that maybe make these opportunities, that make these opportunities understandable and attractive to Scouts?

131
00:09:18,020 --> 00:09:22,300
If they're not understandable and attractive, there's something wrong with the way that we're doing things

132
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:24,420
And that's where we really have to look.

133
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:25,900
I hope that helps, David.

