00:11:04,660 --> 00:11:14,660
In this edition of the podcast in ScoutsmasterCG in 7 minutes or less, we're going to talk about the dual role of being a Scouter and Apparent,

147
00:11:14,660 --> 00:11:20,660
And then we have some email questions to answer. That's going to take up the remainder of the podcast, so let's get started, shall we?

148
00:11:20,660 --> 00:11:25,660
ScoutsmasterCG in 7 minutes or less.

149
00:11:33,660 --> 00:11:39,660
Now, as we noted earlier, most people get involved in Scouting because they have a child involved,

150
00:11:39,660 --> 00:11:46,660
And the dual role of being a Scouter and the parent of a Scout can be a little challenging.

151
00:11:46,660 --> 00:11:49,660
Those challenges are easily outweighed by the reward.

152
00:11:49,660 --> 00:11:55,660
I want to note that I'm using the material that I have in this book, The Scouting Journey, to talk about this subject,

153
00:11:55,660 --> 00:12:03,660
And it is available now in both electronic and you know real book with pages and a cover editions.

154
00:12:03,660 --> 00:12:09,660
I would say that a good parent and a good Scouter both share similar goals for children,

155
00:12:09,660 --> 00:12:19,660
But Scouters aren't going to impose the same expectations or exercise the same authority they have for their own children on Scouts.

156
00:12:19,660 --> 00:12:25,660
And this is where the rub comes in, because your child is also a Scout.

157
00:12:25,660 --> 00:12:31,660
So you have a set of parental expectations and you have a set of Scouting expectations,

158
00:12:31,660 --> 00:12:38,660
I think on average. I think you'll agree with me that parents are probably more demanding and critical and a little more emotional

159
00:12:38,660 --> 00:12:40,660
when they're working with their own child.

160
00:12:40,660 --> 00:12:46,660
Things will go much smoother for you if you prepare your children and yourself

161
00:12:46,660 --> 00:12:53,660
to observe the boundary between these two roles of being a parent and being a Scouter.

162
00:12:53,660 --> 00:12:58,660
You may do and say things as a Scouter that make your children proud.

163
00:12:58,660 --> 00:13:05,660
You may do some things as a Scouter that kind of make them cringe a little bit,

164
00:13:05,660 --> 00:13:08,660
And they will no doubt return the favor for you.

165
00:13:08,660 --> 00:13:14,660
Your child's reasons for being a Scout are different from your reasons for being a Scouter.

166
00:13:14,660 --> 00:13:19,660
You want to spend time with your child and you want to help Scouts in general,

167
00:13:19,660 --> 00:13:24,660
but they are almost always more interested in spending time with their friends.

168
00:13:24,660 --> 00:13:29,660
They're not necessarily in Scouting to spend time with mom or dad.

169
00:13:29,660 --> 00:13:34,660
We need to also understand that parents and children have what I call inside information on each other

170
00:13:34,660 --> 00:13:38,660
that can skew a Scouter's judgment and objectivity.

171
00:13:38,660 --> 00:13:43,660
Both parent and child both know what goes on at home and at school and at work,

172
00:13:43,660 --> 00:13:47,660
and they know each other's history and their strengths and weaknesses.

173
00:13:47,660 --> 00:13:52,660
So having a trusted fellow Scouter who you can talk to frankly

174
00:13:52,660 --> 00:13:56,660
goes a long way to balancing the roles of parent and Scouter.

175
00:13:56,660 --> 00:14:00,660
Of course, you can return the favor by helping them in the same way

176
00:14:00,660 --> 00:14:05,660
when it comes to their balancing their role as a parent and a Scouter with their own child.

177
00:14:05,660 --> 00:14:13,660
As you balance these two roles, your child is also balancing the roles of being your child and a Scout.

178
00:14:13,660 --> 00:14:16,660
So you really have to discuss this together.

179
00:14:16,660 --> 00:14:20,660
You discuss this with your child and agree on some boundaries

180
00:14:20,660 --> 00:14:22,660
and don't switch roles too often.

181
00:14:22,660 --> 00:14:26,660
Don't treat your child as a child one minute and a Scout, the next,

182
00:14:26,660 --> 00:14:31,660
Explain that they need to accept the same responsibility as other Scouts

183
00:14:31,660 --> 00:14:37,660
and you cannot be expected to treat them any differently than you're going to treat other Scouts.

184
00:14:37,660 --> 00:14:42,660
Now, at times, of course, especially with younger Scouts, they're going to need some consolation

185
00:14:42,660 --> 00:14:48,660
or help. only a parent can offer, and it may help to set aside time within a Scouting activity

186
00:14:48,660 --> 00:14:54,660
when you and your child become parent and child, rather than Scouter and Scout just for a time,

187
00:14:54,660 --> 00:14:59,660
and you walk away from the group and you just talk to each other and you address any concerns

188
00:14:59,660 --> 00:15:04,660
and you share your thoughts with one another and then you return to the Scouter and the Scout role.

189
00:15:04,660 --> 00:15:06,660
That helps balance things out a little bit.

190
00:15:06,660 --> 00:15:12,660
As with just about everything else in life, I think, if we understand there may be some challenges ahead,

191
00:15:12,660 --> 00:15:16,660
that if we sit down and we talk to one another and we figure things out

192
00:15:16,660 --> 00:15:21,660
and, like I said, we set some boundaries and we don't switch back between the roles too often,

193
00:15:21,660 --> 00:15:24,660
that that will really smooth things out pretty well.

194
00:15:46,660 --> 00:16:02,660
I need a letter. Send it by name

195
00:16:02,660 --> 00:16:04,660
Email, that is, folks.

196
00:16:04,660 --> 00:16:08,660
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

197
00:16:08,660 --> 00:16:18,660
Well, we've got a pretty full slate of emails to answer here.

198
00:16:18,660 --> 00:16:22,660
Christopher Remke is with Nashville, Tennessee, Troop 6.

199
00:16:22,660 --> 00:16:29,660
And he wrote in to say we are a growing troop and previously used the junior leader training program.

200
00:16:29,660 --> 00:16:36,660
Troop growth is adding to our desire and need to have the patrol method providing the foundations for youth leadership,

201
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:45,660
While the adults also need massive amounts of patience. many Scouts advancing into leadership are happily waiting to see

202
00:16:45,660 --> 00:16:50,660
if others will take care of business, both in planning and execution.

203
00:16:50,660 --> 00:16:54,660
Of course, the adults often break down and provide the support.

204
00:16:54,660 --> 00:17:02,660
The newer BSA program for position education is apparently the Introduction to Outdoor Leadership Skills or ILST.

205
00:17:02,660 --> 00:17:07,659
I'm looking for suggestions for fun, creative ways to use this or a similar program.

206
00:17:07,659 --> 00:17:13,659
Is this the latest program and do you have a best practice or successful program to share?

207
00:17:13,659 --> 00:17:16,659
Well, Chris, I'm happy to hear that the troop is growing

208
00:17:16,659 --> 00:17:25,659
And I'll note that your Scouts are waiting for adults to step in for one really simple reason: because adults are stepping in.

209
00:17:26,660 --> 00:17:33,660
You know, putting the patrol method into practice means you've got to kind of draw the line and not allow adults to cross it.

210
00:17:33,660 --> 00:17:39,660
That's the real challenge and it's just that simple. Scouts are intelligent.

211
00:17:39,660 --> 00:17:44,660
If other people are going to do their work, they're happy to sit by and let other people do the work.

212
00:17:44,660 --> 00:17:48,660
If they're going to have to do it, they will pitch in and do it,

213
00:17:48,660 --> 00:17:52,660
But if somebody's going to come and rescue them, they're smart.

214
00:17:52,660 --> 00:17:57,660
They won't put any effort into something when they know that somebody else is going to do it anyway.

215
00:17:57,660 --> 00:18:04,660
The only way that you get to the point where they're taking the responsibility is making sure that nobody else picks it up.

216
00:18:04,660 --> 00:18:08,660
It'll cause a little bit of difficulty and it'll be a little challenging at first,

217
00:18:08,660 --> 00:18:13,660
But once the idea spreads that hey, if you don't do this, nobody else is,

218
00:18:13,660 --> 00:18:17,660
they'll begin taking those responsibilities more seriously.

219
00:18:17,660 --> 00:18:25,660
I think that the junior leader training syllabus and the ILST syllabus- I think they're all fine-

220
00:18:25,660 --> 00:18:28,660
I go about things in a little bit of a different way.

221
00:18:28,660 --> 00:18:36,660
My premise is that youth leaders don't need training so much as they need to know what to do next,

222
00:18:36,660 --> 00:18:41,660
Something that they kind of understand anyway, but they just need a little help with it.

223
00:18:41,660 --> 00:18:47,660
Hours of adult-led training sessions send a clear message that adults are in charge

224
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:51,660
and get to decide who does what and how it is done.

225
00:18:51,660 --> 00:18:56,660
Now some guidance and coaching from the scout master is a lot more effective,

226
00:18:56,660 --> 00:18:59,660
And we do this in small doses,

227
00:18:59,660 --> 00:19:03,660
In a few minutes here and there usually does the trick.

228
00:19:03,660 --> 00:19:06,660
Let them discover what works and what doesn't work.

229
00:19:06,660 --> 00:19:13,660
Give them a few basic guidelines and I would send you to a post that we wrote within the past several weeks

230
00:19:13,660 --> 00:19:19,660
about 15-minute patrol leader training, and work with them as they figure things out.

231
00:19:19,660 --> 00:19:27,660
Now this will mean that at first you're going to move with glacial slowness as scouts begin to get the idea,

232
00:19:27,660 --> 00:19:34,660
And your fellow scouters may be incredibly frustrated and impatient when it looks like things are falling apart.

233
00:19:34,660 --> 00:19:42,660
But if you look hard enough, you'll see scouts actually leading things and actually making things happen.

234
00:19:42,660 --> 00:19:50,660
If you are going to have a training session on youth leadership, I think it's better to spend that time training adults rather than scouts.

235
00:19:50,660 --> 00:19:57,660
I really don't recommend any training syllabus or having special training for youth leadership. as I said before,

236
00:19:57,660 --> 00:20:01,660
Do it a little bit at a time over a period of weeks, as they need it.

237
00:20:01,660 --> 00:20:09,660
Stick right with them. Show them what to do. Sometimes you have to actually step in and do it and then turn around and say:

238
00:20:09,660 --> 00:20:12,660
did you see what I did there? That's what you do.

239
00:20:12,660 --> 00:20:18,660
Leadership is not something that you can train into somebody. It can only be developed over time.

240
00:20:18,660 --> 00:20:26,660
Adults tend to over explain and talk way too much about things scouts don't really need to know at that very moment.

241
00:20:27,660 --> 00:20:33,660
So we're doing things as needed. What's happening next? That's what they need to know,

242
00:20:33,660 --> 00:20:38,660
And then they need to know what to do and maybe a little bit of how to do it.

243
00:20:38,660 --> 00:20:46,660
But basically the idea is to let them go, coach them along until they begin to get the idea.

244
00:20:46,660 --> 00:20:54,660
Think about riding a bike. We wouldn't sit a child down and give them a long PowerPoint presentation about riding a bike.

245
00:20:54,660 --> 00:21:01,660
Nope, We put them on the bike, We stand right there next to them, We let them pedal it for a little while.

246
00:21:01,660 --> 00:21:05,660
We talk about balance and things like that. We may even have training wheels on the bike,

247
00:21:05,660 --> 00:21:14,660
but we give them the chance to do that a little bit and then gradually let go and pretty soon they're riding the bike.

248
00:21:14,660 --> 00:21:17,660
That's the kind of thing that I'm talking about,

249
00:21:17,660 --> 00:21:25,660
And I will say again: leadership is learned by doing things, not by listening to somebody talk about what it's like to be a leader.

250
00:21:25,660 --> 00:21:32,660
My best advice, based on my experience, is to work with scouts on the thing that's happening next,

251
00:21:32,660 --> 00:21:38,660
Help them through it, And then you can also begin to layer in the idea of:

252
00:21:38,660 --> 00:21:42,660
well, we need to not only talk about what's happening in the next 10 minutes,

253
00:21:42,660 --> 00:21:47,660
but we need to look at the next hour, the next day, the next week, the next month, the next year

254
00:21:47,660 --> 00:21:50,660
And they'll begin to get the idea.

255
00:21:50,660 --> 00:21:53,660
Thanks, Chris, for being in touch And I hope that helps.

256
00:21:53,660 --> 00:22:00,660
I had this email that I'm going to let remain anonymous because it's a bit of a ticklish situation.

257
00:22:00,660 --> 00:22:05,660
The listener wrote in to say I need advice on how to handle a disruptive parent.

258
00:22:05,660 --> 00:22:09,660
The family has been recently divorced and it didn't go all that well

259
00:22:09,660 --> 00:22:11,660
And there is split custody.

260
00:22:11,660 --> 00:22:15,660
So I deal with the father every other week at a scout meeting.

261
00:22:15,660 --> 00:22:17,660
The father is disruptive.

262
00:22:17,660 --> 00:22:23,660
He answers his phone in the middle of the meeting, uses vulgar language allowed enough for other parents

263
00:22:23,660 --> 00:22:25,660
And I am sure children to hear,

264
00:22:25,660 --> 00:22:28,660
And he continues to question my ability.

265
00:22:28,660 --> 00:22:34,660
I have talked with the scouts mother and explained that if this behavior continues,

266
00:22:34,660 --> 00:22:37,660
dad is not going to be allowed to bring the scout again.

267
00:22:37,660 --> 00:22:42,660
Her response was that his behavior was not all that surprising to her,

268
00:22:42,660 --> 00:22:48,660
because the scout has been thrown out of other activities because of his father's actions.

269
00:22:48,660 --> 00:22:51,660
I talked to the other adult leadership of our unit

270
00:22:51,660 --> 00:22:58,660
And the only advice they gave me was to tread lightly, because this is such a ticklish situation.

271
00:22:58,660 --> 00:23:00,660
We don't want to make it worse for the scout.

272
00:23:00,660 --> 00:23:02,660
I honestly feel stuck.

273
00:23:02,660 --> 00:23:06,660
This behavior is distracting and disrespectful and inappropriate.

274
00:23:06,660 --> 00:23:10,660
What's the best way to approach this without the child's suffering?

275
00:23:10,660 --> 00:23:13,660
Well, this is indeed a difficult situation

276
00:23:13,660 --> 00:23:15,660
And you do have the right focus:

277
00:23:15,660 --> 00:23:20,660
Not trying to do more harm to the scout who is involved in this

278
00:23:20,660 --> 00:23:23,660
Now, not actually seeing or hearing what's going on,

279
00:23:23,660 --> 00:23:26,660
It's difficult for me to judge what direction you should take.

280
00:23:27,660 --> 00:23:31,660
If you decide that dad has to go, then the unit leadership,

281
00:23:31,660 --> 00:23:36,660
not just you, but you want the unit leader and the committee chair.

282
00:23:36,660 --> 00:23:41,660
they need to absolutely back you up and meet with this father face to face,

283
00:23:41,660 --> 00:23:43,660
out of hearing of the scouts,

284
00:23:43,660 --> 00:23:47,660
No emails, no phone calls. face to face meeting.

285
00:23:47,660 --> 00:23:50,660
It may be at the next meeting where he brings the scout.

286
00:23:50,660 --> 00:23:54,660
It may be that they need to set up a special time and go meet with him,

287
00:23:54,660 --> 00:23:59,660
but they need to tell him in no uncertain terms that his behavior

288
00:23:59,660 --> 00:24:02,660
and his attitude simply will not be tolerated.

289
00:24:02,660 --> 00:24:06,660
Naturally, this is a very unpleasant task for anyone,

290
00:24:06,660 --> 00:24:11,660
especially someone who is just a volunteer and is trying to be helpful,

291
00:24:11,660 --> 00:24:14,660
But sometimes we all need to have a little backbone

292
00:24:14,660 --> 00:24:16,660
and stand up for each other.

293
00:24:16,660 --> 00:24:18,660
Now these folks who are going to address this with dad

294
00:24:18,660 --> 00:24:20,660
need to think through what they're going to say

295
00:24:20,660 --> 00:24:22,660
and then they need to make it stick.

296
00:24:22,660 --> 00:24:25,660
There's no argument to be had, there's no equivocation.

297
00:24:25,660 --> 00:24:29,660
They tell dad that this is unacceptable

298
00:24:29,660 --> 00:24:33,660
and that if he chooses to act this way, that's it.

299
00:24:33,660 --> 00:24:36,660
He won't be able to participate with his son

300
00:24:36,660 --> 00:24:38,660
Again. this is not very pleasant,

301
00:24:38,660 --> 00:24:41,660
but you can't let things like this go on.

302
00:24:41,660 --> 00:24:43,660
I have had to do this sort of thing in the past

303
00:24:43,660 --> 00:24:47,660
and I have learned that quick, definitive and authoritative action

304
00:24:47,660 --> 00:24:49,660
is what resolves the problem.

305
00:24:49,660 --> 00:24:52,660
I'm sorry you find yourself in such a distressing situation

306
00:24:52,660 --> 00:24:55,660
and I hope that this helps, and do let me know

307
00:24:55,660 --> 00:24:57,660
if I can be of any further assistance.

308
00:24:57,660 --> 00:25:00,660
I also recently heard from Chris, who had this to say:

309
00:25:00,660 --> 00:25:05,660
I will be the scout master of a troop of nearly 100 scouts next month.

310
00:25:05,660 --> 00:25:09,660
We have a wonderful troop and an active set of parents and scouts.

311
00:25:09,660 --> 00:25:13,660
However, our success and size is leading to some challenges.

312
00:25:13,660 --> 00:25:17,660
For example, we traditionally have had about five patrols.

313
00:25:17,660 --> 00:25:20,660
That means we have a senior patrol leader,

314
00:25:20,660 --> 00:25:22,660
three or four assistance senior patrol leaders

315
00:25:22,660 --> 00:25:24,660
and five patrol leaders.

316
00:25:24,660 --> 00:25:28,660
Most of these are 13, 14-year-old scouts

317
00:25:28,660 --> 00:25:31,660
and our patrol size is pretty large.

318
00:25:31,660 --> 00:25:36,660
On campouts we only usually have 30 or 40 scouts show up.

319
00:25:36,660 --> 00:25:38,660
So we do wind up with manageable patrols

320
00:25:38,660 --> 00:25:42,660
of about six or eight boys apiece. on campouts

321
00:25:42,660 --> 00:25:45,660
We can add more patrols to increase the number of patrol leaders

322
00:25:45,660 --> 00:25:47,660
and add assistance senior patrol leaders.

323
00:25:47,660 --> 00:25:50,660
But I was wondering if there are other strategies

324
00:25:50,660 --> 00:25:54,660
that large troops use to better handle the challenges of their size.

325
00:25:54,660 --> 00:25:57,660
Well, Chris, if I'm reading this right,

326
00:25:57,660 --> 00:26:01,660
it sounds like you have maybe 15 to 20 scouts in a patrol,

327
00:26:01,660 --> 00:26:03,660
and that's a pretty big crowd.

328
00:26:03,660 --> 00:26:07,660
The idea of patrols is scouts working in the natural gang of friends

329
00:26:07,660 --> 00:26:10,660
on a scale where the real work of scouting is done,

330
00:26:10,660 --> 00:26:13,660
And honestly I can't imagine that happening that well

331
00:26:13,660 --> 00:26:16,660
if the patrol numbers 15 or 20 boys.

332
00:26:16,660 --> 00:26:18,660
Now, since you asked, here's what I would do.

333
00:26:18,660 --> 00:26:20,660
I would tell your senior patrol leader

334
00:26:20,660 --> 00:26:22,660
and the patrol leader's counsel

335
00:26:22,660 --> 00:26:25,660
that they can arrange their patrols as they see fit

336
00:26:25,660 --> 00:26:28,660
and that they should aim for eight to 10 scouts in a patrol.

337
00:26:28,660 --> 00:26:30,660
That's a workable size.

338
00:26:30,660 --> 00:26:33,660
Have a discussion with them about your concerns

339
00:26:33,660 --> 00:26:36,660
that you want to reduce the scale of things down to a level

340
00:26:36,660 --> 00:26:38,660
where scouts are getting the attention

341
00:26:38,660 --> 00:26:40,660
that they need from their patrol leaders

342
00:26:40,660 --> 00:26:42,660
so they don't feel as though they're lost in a big herd

343
00:26:42,660 --> 00:26:44,660
And that they feel that they're part of something

344
00:26:44,660 --> 00:26:46,660
that's important and vital.

345
00:26:46,660 --> 00:26:48,660
And then let them at it.

346
00:26:48,660 --> 00:26:51,660
Get out of the room and see what solutions they come up with.

347
00:26:51,660 --> 00:26:54,660
Once they've formed new patrols, they can elect patrol leaders

348
00:26:54,660 --> 00:26:57,660
and maybe you'll have a few more patrol leaders.

349
00:26:57,660 --> 00:27:00,660
Ask them how they would handle the leadership structure.

350
00:27:00,660 --> 00:27:02,660
I don't really understand what five or six

351
00:27:02,660 --> 00:27:04,660
assistant senior patrol leaders would have to do.

352
00:27:04,660 --> 00:27:07,660
Maybe some of them actually just become patrol leaders

353
00:27:07,660 --> 00:27:10,660
if the newly formed patrols elect them.

354
00:27:10,660 --> 00:27:13,660
Let the patrol leaders counsel, sit down and sort things out.

355
00:27:13,660 --> 00:27:16,660
They may have a question or two for you,

356
00:27:16,660 --> 00:27:19,660
but don't start out by giving them directions.

357
00:27:19,660 --> 00:27:22,660
Let them work things out and then discuss their plans with them.

358
00:27:22,660 --> 00:27:25,660
Do they think they have one big troop,

359
00:27:25,660 --> 00:27:28,660
Or maybe they ought to think about the idea of having two troops?

360
00:27:28,660 --> 00:27:31,660
Put everything on the table and see what they have to say.

361
00:27:31,660 --> 00:27:33,660
Then you respond to what they want

362
00:27:33,660 --> 00:27:35,660
and you do your best to make it happen.

363
00:27:35,660 --> 00:27:38,660
At the end of the day, they have thought things through,

364
00:27:38,660 --> 00:27:41,660
tried some new ideas and developed something for themselves.

365
00:27:41,660 --> 00:27:44,660
Older scouts stay around and stay involved

366
00:27:44,660 --> 00:27:46,660
when they actually get to do things like this,

367
00:27:46,660 --> 00:27:49,660
When they are really leading and making decisions.

368
00:27:49,660 --> 00:27:53,660
Why we have patrols is so scouts get to do what they do with their friends.

369
00:27:53,660 --> 00:27:56,660
It's not about manageability or taking a giant group

370
00:27:56,660 --> 00:27:59,660
and cutting it into smaller groups making it easier to work with.

371
00:27:59,660 --> 00:28:02,660
They ought to be able to figure out who their friends are

372
00:28:02,660 --> 00:28:05,660
better than anybody else and what they want to do

373
00:28:05,660 --> 00:28:07,660
as a patrol better than we can.

374
00:28:07,660 --> 00:28:09,660
Chris, I hope that advice answers your question

375
00:28:09,660 --> 00:28:11,660
and it helps you move forward.

376
00:28:11,660 --> 00:28:14,660
I also received this anonymous question:

377
00:28:14,660 --> 00:28:17,660
Is the blue card for merit badges required?

378
00:28:17,660 --> 00:28:19,660
I've asked other leaders in my troop

379
00:28:19,660 --> 00:28:23,660
if they knew about blue cards and all I got was a blank stare.

380
00:28:23,660 --> 00:28:25,660
Apparently, our troop has never used them.

381
00:28:25,660 --> 00:28:28,660
I looked in the guide to advancement 2013

382
00:28:28,660 --> 00:28:30,660
and they are required,

383
00:28:30,660 --> 00:28:33,660
But how did our troop not even know about them?

384
00:28:33,660 --> 00:28:35,660
Well, I can't really answer

385
00:28:35,660 --> 00:28:37,660
how the troop did not even know about them,

386
00:28:37,660 --> 00:28:39,660
But blue cards are required

387
00:28:39,660 --> 00:28:42,660
and it is in the guide to advancement 2013

388
00:28:42,660 --> 00:28:44,660
and I'm reading directly from that.

389
00:28:44,660 --> 00:28:47,660
It says- though it's not been clearly stated in the past:

390
00:28:47,660 --> 00:28:50,660
units, districts and local councils

391
00:28:50,660 --> 00:28:54,660
do not have the authority to implement a different system

392
00:28:54,660 --> 00:28:57,660
for merit badge approval and documentation

393
00:28:57,660 --> 00:29:00,660
other than the merit badge application or the blue card.

394
00:29:00,660 --> 00:29:02,660
My bet, I guess,

395
00:29:02,660 --> 00:29:05,660
is that someone in the dim, dark past decided that they knew better

396
00:29:05,660 --> 00:29:07,660
and dispensed with blue cards

397
00:29:07,660 --> 00:29:09,660
and they thought they had a better system.

398
00:29:09,660 --> 00:29:12,660
But the BSA is pretty clear in this matter

399
00:29:12,660 --> 00:29:15,660
in expressing policy that nobody has the authority

400
00:29:15,660 --> 00:29:17,660
to implement a different system

401
00:29:17,660 --> 00:29:20,660
for merit badge approval and documentation

402
00:29:20,660 --> 00:29:23,660
than the actual application for a merit badge,

403
00:29:23,660 --> 00:29:27,660
which is commonly known as the blue card.

404
00:29:27,660 --> 00:29:29,660
So I hope that helps.

405
00:29:29,660 --> 00:29:31,660
If you have a question or a conundrum,

406
00:29:31,660 --> 00:29:33,660
get in touch with me and you're going to find out

407
